From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kasey Prichard <kasey.prichard@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 04 Oct 2026 23:50:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] ASoC: tas2783-sdw: do not block system resume on
 peripheral re-attach

tas2783_sdca_dev_resume() serves both runtime and system resume, and
starts by waiting up to TAS2783_PROBE_TIMEOUT (5 s) for the peripheral
to be enumerated and initialised again. For system resume that wait runs
in the PM core's device resume phase, before tasks are thawed, so the
whole machine waits with it.

On the HP OmniBook Ultra 14 (board 8EB4, Panther Lake, four TAS2783, two
each on SoundWire links 1 and 2) the wait cannot be met. After the
manager resets the bus, tas_update_status() resets each amplifier and
downloads its 40 KiB firmware again, and sdw_handle_slave_status()
initialises the peripherals on a link one after the other. The same two
amplifiers are late every time: sdw:0:1:0102:0000:01:a on every resume,
and sdw:0:2:0102:0000:01:9 on most. They are not ready when their
callback gives up, and the timeouts run back to back. Kernel timestamps
from one resume on 7.2.5, relative to "ACPI: EC: interrupt unblocked":

   +0.16 s  nvme nvme0: 16/0/0 default/read/poll queues
   +5.87 s  slave-tas2783 sdw:0:1:0102:0000:01:a: Initialization not complete
            ... PM: failed to resume: error -110
  +11.51 s  slave-tas2783 sdw:0:2:0102:0000:01:9: Initialization not complete
            ... PM: failed to resume: error -110
  +12.13 s  Restarting tasks: Done

Six resumes over three boots (7.1.9 and 7.2.5) stalled for 10 to 13 s
this way; four resumes of the same 7.2.5 kernel without the amplifiers
working took a second or less. The lock screen is on the panel the whole
time and takes no input. The late amplifiers do attach afterwards, so
the wait bought nothing: the callback had already failed.

Give system resume its own callback. When the manager reset the bus
(slave->unattach_request), return without waiting: tas_update_status()
already does everything the peripheral needs when it attaches. It takes
the regmap out of cache-only mode, drops the stale cache, resets the
device, downloads the firmware and writes the init sequence. When the
bus was not reset the peripheral is still attached, the wait completes
immediately, and behaviour is unchanged. Runtime resume is unchanged
too.

The amplifiers become usable at the same moment as before. What changes
is that user space is running while they finish, so a stream opened in
that window can fail ("error playback without fw download") where
before the whole system waited.

Link: https://github.com/omacom/omarchy/issues/14172
Link: https://github.com/thesofproject/linux/issues/5732
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
---
Not signed off. Tested on 7.2.5-3-omarchy over one short suspend/resume
cycle: kernel resume 0.6 s (was 10 to 13 s), the four amplifiers
re-initialised 2.5, 2.5, 5.1 and 5.1 s after their resume callback, and
playback worked afterwards. Add your Signed-off-by once it has been
through more cycles, including a long sleep.

Cut against linux-omarchy 7.2.5-3, whose driver matches sound for-next
here. It depends on b627da430357 ("ASoC: tas2783-sdw: drop stale
regcache on uninitialized re-attach"), which linux-omarchy carries and
plain v7.2.5 does not: without it the cache this callback no longer
syncs is left stale instead of dropped.

 sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c | 34 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c
@@ -1157,8 +1157,40 @@ static s32 tas2783_sdca_dev_resume(struc
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static s32 tas2783_sdca_dev_system_resume(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct sdw_slave *slave = dev_to_sdw_dev(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * The manager did not reset the bus, so the peripheral never left it
+	 * and the wait in the common path completes at once.
+	 */
+	if (!slave->unattach_request)
+		return tas2783_sdca_dev_resume(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * The manager reset the bus on its way back up and the peripheral is
+	 * re-enumerating. Once it attaches, tas_update_status() takes the
+	 * regmap out of cache-only mode, resets the device and downloads its
+	 * firmware again, which takes seconds per amplifier. The bus handles
+	 * the peripherals on a link one after the other, so with two
+	 * amplifiers on a link the second one may still be waiting its turn
+	 * when TAS2783_PROBE_TIMEOUT expires. Waiting for it here holds up
+	 * the whole system resume, once per late amplifier, only to fail the
+	 * callback with -ETIMEDOUT.
+	 *
+	 * Nothing after the wait is needed in this case either, since
+	 * tas_update_status() drops the cache that would be synced. Let the
+	 * amplifiers come back in the background instead.
+	 */
+	slave->unattach_request = 0;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static const struct dev_pm_ops tas2783_sdca_pm = {
-	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(tas2783_sdca_dev_system_suspend, tas2783_sdca_dev_resume)
+	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(tas2783_sdca_dev_system_suspend,
+			    tas2783_sdca_dev_system_resume)
 	RUNTIME_PM_OPS(tas2783_sdca_dev_suspend, tas2783_sdca_dev_resume, NULL)
 };
 
